      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A={2,4,8}.$ \n\n$$\nR={(2,2),(2,4),(2,8),(4,2),(4,4),(4,8)}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nIn this relation, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y){{field-1}}R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z){{field-2}}R\\,⟹\\,{{field-3}}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is \n{{field-4}}.\n\nFor example, since $(2,4)$ and $(4,8)$ belong to $R,$ transitivity of $R$ requires \n{{field-5}}\n$\\in R.$",
      "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\n- Consider the pair $(2,4)$ whose second component is $4.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $4:$ \n\n- Linking $(2,4)$ with $(4,2)$ requires that $(2,2)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Linking $(2,4)$ with $(4,8)$ requires that $(2,8)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Consider the pair $(2,8)$ whose second component is $8.$ It can't be linked with any pair from $R$ since none of them have a first component of $8.$\n\n- Consider the pair $(4,2)$ whose second component is $2.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $2:$ \n\n- Linking $(4,2)$ with $(2,4)$ requires that $(4,4)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Linking $(4,2)$ with $(2,8)$ requires that $(4,8)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Consider the pair $(4,8)$ whose second component is $8.$ It can't be linked with any pair from $R$ since none of them have a first component of $8.$\n\nSo, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is  $\\text{transitive}.$",
